In the meantime, #swedengate is about much more than the lack of dinner invitations. In one post, for example, it is complained that Sweden not only lets children go hungry, but that "racist ice cream" is also being sold in the country. This refers to a popsicle with the name "88" – the number combination is also frequently used by Nazis because it is said to symbolize "Heil Hitler." Other internet users recalled, under the hashtag, the debate surrounding the former "N-word buns," now called "Chokladboller," a Swedish chocolate confection. The reaction from the Right did not take long to arrive. Both Swedish and German Right-wingers described reports from [Gruppe 1] as exaggerated whining. Others were of the opinion that supporters of the Russian President Vladimir Putin had created the hashtag. In the meantime, even the "Psychological Defence Agency" in Sweden has investigated the case and ruled out a targeted influence campaign.

While the case is certainly being taken seriously in Sweden, it is causing amusement internationally. The British newspaper "The Guardian" headlined "It’s a Scandi-Scandal" and approached the internet phenomenon in a rather ironic way. It quoted the Swede Linda Johannson, who wrote in an opinion piece in "The Independent" that the tradition really does exist – but as a child, she hadn't cared about not getting to eat anything at friends' houses."
}

