{
  "label": "Will the sandy beaches therefore soon be completely swallowed by the sea? It is not that simple, says Lasse Sander. He considers the Vousdoukas study to be reductive and alarmist because it undermines the natural complexity of coasts. That sandy beaches worldwide will simply disappear when the water rises is not quite correct. There are already huge regional differences, as can be seen in the North Sea. Sylt is heavily exposed to the waves and would actually migrate to the east. The Danish neighbor island Rømø, on the other hand, has so much sand that approaching storms and the rising sea level can hardly do anything to it. Beach nourishment is not necessary there.

The methodology of such global studies is also under criticism. They are calculated using the so-called Bruun Rule from the 1950s. This tool of coastal engineering states in simplified terms: if the sea level rises, the beach profile becomes steeper and the sand is eroded. However, geomorphologists consider this rule to be detached from reality; eleven authors around [Gruppe 1] Andrew Cooper therefore sharply contradicted the Vousdoukas study in a rebuttal in Nature Climate Change. If the sea level rises, sandy beaches would migrate landward, they wrote. At least in places where there is space to retreat.

Geomorphologist Wenyan Zhang from the Helmholtz-Zentrum Hereon in Geesthacht sees a good data basis for both approaches. However, he tends to agree with Cooper, as a simplified method for estimating global change cannot provide reliable or meaningful results. Furthermore, most coasts worldwide are now being changed by humans and not by natural processes, says Zhang, which is always neglected in studies on a global level. An analysis of satellite-based coastline data from 1984 to 2016 showed that 24 percent of the world's sandy coasts are actually eroding, 48 percent are stable, but 28 percent are growing. In the mid-80s, a study concluded that 70 percent of the world's sandy coasts were subject to erosion. According to this, sandy coast erosion has eased on a global level. Wenyan Zhang attributes this trend largely to coastal protection. Beach nourishment has been intensified in recent decades."
