  "label": "Sea Blockade vs. Harbor Blockade

In Odesa, the [Andere Gruppe] are facing the [Gruppe 1] with a harbor blockade. They have laid sea mines in the water to prevent Russia from capturing the port of Ukraine's third-largest city. "This involves so-called anchor mines. These are mines that are laid in shallower waters just off ports or coastlines. They essentially serve to prevent an amphibious landing," explains military expert Wolfgang Richter from the Stiftung Wissenschaft und Politik (SWP) in the ntv podcast "Wieder was gelernt".

Anchor mines are attached to an anchor by a rope. The metal spheres lurk just below the water's surface. If a ship touches the mine, it explodes and usually causes the ship to sink. It is unclear how many of these explosives are lying in the Black Sea. However, from a military strategic perspective, such a process is not unusual, says Wolfgang Richter. Sea mines are an effective means of preventing an adversary from attacking coastal cities from the sea. "With the mines, you stop the enemy; you force them into mine clearance operations. During this time, you can shell the enemy ships from the coast. For this purpose, the [Andere Gruppe] have appropriately positioned long-range artillery and anti-ship missiles, for example of the 'Neptun' type," explains Richter."
