Carpano is an alternative to the now-inflationary Aperol. At least when speaking about the history of the custom of consuming an alcoholic drink in the early evening to bridge the time until dinner. While [Gruppe 1] and [Andere Gruppe] raise their hands equally quickly when it comes to the question of the invention of the aperitif, it is only considered certain that the custom originates from the Mediterranean region and likely began with wine-based beverages such as vermouth. Turin, in particular, is touted as the birthplace of the aperitif, and one would like to believe it when seeing with what passion the city's residents populate the bars there in the evening, such as the Caffé Mulassano in the old town at Piazza Castello.

In this corner of Turin, the spirits merchant and coffeehouse owner Antonio Benedetto Carpano is said to have invented a sweetened, fortified "luxury wine" at the end of the 18th century, which he aromatized with, among other things, the medicinal herb vermouth. Due to its bitter substances—hence the term "wormwood drop"—it had already been praised by Hippocrates as a "miracle cure" more than 2,000 years earlier. The tonic was used against worm infestations or for stomach problems and was a known term in Italy as "Vermouth." It was only through Carpano that it became a beverage for enjoyment. The "punt e mes" variant is still particularly popular today, because in the past, vermouth was mostly drunk mixed, with vanilla or additional bitter substances. For instance, a stockbroker in 1870 is said to have once ordered the Carpano "punt e mes" (one and a half), one part vermouth, one half part bitter."
