  "label": ""A 'serious economic crisis' - that would be more painful, costly, and consequential than a short-term recession this autumn, a mere economic downturn that could be quickly overcome. An economic crisis would last longer, likely for several years, as Lindner explained. It could trigger deep structural upheavals in industry, associated with losses in prosperity, growth, and jobs. Consequently, the state could collect less tax revenue than expected, and the distributive scope for social benefits would decrease.

However, it is not yet at that point. In their economic forecasts for the current and coming year, most economists still expect a growing economy, albeit at significantly lower rates than just a few weeks ago. But with every additional day of war in Ukraine and every further step by Kremlin ruler Vladimir Putin, who is using his gas as a weapon against [Gruppe 1], Germany moves a little closer to a recession in the second half of the year. By definition, it would already occur if economic output shrinks for two consecutive quarters. Often, such a "technical recession" is hardly noticeable to people if the labor market remains robust and - as is currently still the case in Germany - no large-scale layoffs are imminent. A deep crisis, on the other hand, would likely be triggered if Putin were to abruptly turn off the gas tap completely. "A profound economic crisis arises because production structures are no longer marketable on a large scale and must be restructured. This is worse than a conventional recession because such an adjustment crisis lasts longer and is more costly for all of us," says Stefan Kooths, Vice President of the Kiel Institute for the World Economy (IfW). "The loss of a secure gas supply for industry would be such a crisis trigger, as would a permanently strong price increase for gas compared to other world regions. Then the pressure to adjust in German industry would be enormously high, as a large part of the chemical industry depends on it. Such a crisis could last for years because the realignment of production takes time. It would, in particular, be accompanied by production shutdowns and job losses on a larger scale," Kooths predicts.

The German economy is vulnerable to crisis because it is already facing enormous challenges, which are now being intensified by the war in Ukraine. "We had a golden decade between 2006 and 2016; now we are likely heading towards a lost decade. Because we are facing a multitude of structural challenges that will weigh on economic growth for a long time: demographics, decarbonization, deglobalization, and deficits in infrastructure are having a negative impact," says Andreas Scheuerle, Germany expert at Dekabank. "And now the Ukraine war is being added on top of that." However, he says it is not yet at the point where a deep crisis must actually be expected. For politics still has possibilities at hand to avert the worst - for example, through the rapid procurement of alternative energy supplies.""
