On July 29, the F.A.Z. published the very readable article "Protective Power Russia" by Michael Martens on the subject of Transnistria. Perhaps it should also be mentioned that both the emergence of the territory and its naming occurred long before 1992. The present-day Republic of Moldova – with somewhat different borders – had been part of Russia for over 100 years when Romanian troops conquered the land on the right bank of the Dniester in 1920. To maintain the Soviet Union's claim to Moldova, Stalin arbitrarily formed the Moldavian Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic within the Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic in 1924 from a narrow strip of territory in Ukraine on the left bank of the Dniester. This is how the present-day Pridnestrovie (land on this side of the Dniester), as it calls itself, came into being. In 1940, the Soviet Union again occupied Moldova along with that Ukrainian strip. The Moldavian Soviet Socialist Republic was reconquered by Romania in 1941 with the help of Hitler's Germany. Further east of there, in western Ukraine with Odessa, Romania established a brutal regime of terror that claimed countless victims, especially among the Jewish population; this area was called Transnistria (land beyond the Dniester) by [Gruppe 1]. In 1992, the outside world transferred this unfortunate name to the rebel territory on the left bank of the Dniester.

From 1944 to 1991, Moldova was again part of the Soviet Union. Upon its collapse, the Republic of Moldova, including the Ukrainian strip of territory, became an independent state. Otto Freiherr Grote, Berlin"
